[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Build a playable core first\n- Treat 0-move starts, near-zero card usage, and universal unwinnability as structural failures before difficulty problems. First restore a legal, coherent core loop; only then tune challenge.\n- Guarantee broad opening mobility across many shuffles. A solitaire needs at least one common early move, usually via a simple build rule, movement to empty spaces, or another low-friction transfer that the initial layout can actually satisfy.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Impossible suit/color tests, unsupported setup directives, contradictory predicates, or source/destination references the engine cannot realize can make a design functionally dead even if it looks close on paper.\n- Make the move graph connected. Every populated pile type should either send cards toward progress or feed a helper pile that does; piles named in the objective must be reachable and changeable through legal play.\n- If a large share of cards starts in stock/draw, that source must actually expose cards into play. Draw-gating rules should be used sparingly and should not block the game\u2019s main pacing mechanism at the source itself.\n\n## Align card flow with the win condition\n- A win condition should measure resolution of the actual play area, not mere depletion of a bookkeeping source like stock/draw. If wins happen with very low pile or card usage, the objective is probably bypassing the intended game.\n- If the win requires piles to empty, the rules need a genuine sink: foundations, removals, completed-run cash-outs, or another irreversible outlet. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying objective.\n- Every major component should have a real role in victory. If a pile type appears in setup but has no meaningful moves, or can receive cards but not release them, it becomes dead weight, a pointless sink, or an accidental blocker.\n- Pile roles should be clear and consistent: source piles expose cards, tableau reorganizes them, and at least one destination type accumulates durable progress. Designs become more coherent when victory matches those roles.\n\n## Control state-space shape\n- Search-cap losses often mean the game is ill-posed, not just hard: either productive moves are missing, or there are too many reversible, low-value moves. Diagnose structure before adjusting numbers.\n- Prefer a small set of coherent progression rules over exotic or overly broad movement. Clear build relationships create strategy and solvability better than permissive moves that only increase branching.\n- When legal moves are plentiful but aimless, tighten the highest-volume routes so destinations encode meaningful structure. This usually improves both decision quality and bot solvability more than adding new freedoms.\n- When legal moves are too scarce, add one broadly useful primitive action rather than many special cases. A single circulation or consolidation move often repairs deadness more effectively than a large rewrite.\n- Avoid unrestricted holding areas. Piles that accept cards easily but do not advance victory or release cards cleanly tend to create large, unproductive search spaces.\n\n## Design for relevance, not loopholes\n- Tighten trivial objectives before adding mechanics. If the game wins in identical short lines or even at 0 moves, fix the win condition first so the main tableau and active piles must matter.\n- If some piles or cards remain mostly unused in successful play, reconnect them to the objective before increasing complexity. Requiring existing play areas to participate is usually stronger than adding new subsystems.\n- Make at least one destination type a long-term home for completed work. Without an explicit completion pattern, solitaire tends to produce churn instead of progress.\n- Early repair passes should be minimal and informative: preserve the intended interaction, but normalize the rules into a compact, internally consistent system that can reveal real difficulty and decision-making.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a bot consistently hits search limits with high card usage, the problem is often excessive reversible freedom rather than insufficient moves. Tightening the most common move so it follows the same structural pattern as stack movement can reduce churn and make progress legible without removing the game's core interaction.\n2. If every opening deal is a 0-move loss, fix the move graph before tuning difficulty: add one broadly available tableau action and one irreversible progress sink that the initial layout can actually feed. Also, a pile type that can only receive cards should usually be paired with a win condition that treats it as completed work, not as a temporary holding area.\n3. When a rules engine forbids duplicate source/destination move definitions, merge related permissions into one OR tree instead of expressing them as separate rules. Also, repair syntax and structural legality before tuning difficulty; a minimally changed, compilable ruleset gives far more useful playtest feedback than a speculative redesign.\n4. When a solitaire fails to compile because cards are unassigned, the best next step is usually a minimal arithmetic correction rather than a broader redesign. Restore a legal setup first so later playtest results reflect gameplay structure instead of bookkeeping errors.\n5. When a mostly-solvable tableau game still has occasional proven 0- or 1-move losses, the problem is often opening mobility rather than overall difficulty. A narrow empty-space rule such as \u201conly Kings to empties\u201d can repair dead starts while preserving structure better than making all empty moves free.\n6. When a solitaire loses at the search cap with moderate-to-high card usage, the problem is often not lack of moves but lack of irreversible progress. Converting \u201call-at-once completion\u201d into a steadier sink can shrink unproductive branching while keeping the same overall objective.\n7. If a win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the game needs an irreversible sink that actually removes or locks progress; otherwise rearrangement alone cannot finish the job. When fixing a broken solitaire, first ensure every card is accounted for and every destination pile has a clear role in the path to victory.\n8. If a solitaire asks the player to empty piles, it needs an irreversible sink that actively accepts cards from the main play area; otherwise the game becomes churn rather than progress. When search caps are caused by too many reversible moves, tightening the highest-traffic tableau rule often helps more than adding even more mobility.\n9. When a solitaire prototype fails to compile or has no legal move loop, fix arithmetic and establish one simple source\u2192tableau\u2192sink progression before tuning difficulty. A minimal coherent ruleset reveals real playtest signals much better than adding complexity to a broken setup.\n10. If the objective is to empty tableau piles, the game needs an irreversible destination that actually consumes cards; otherwise even rich mobility just creates a large reversible search space. When a bot repeatedly hits search limits with moderate pile usage, replacing a generic holding pile with a structured sink often helps more than adding extra movement freedom.\n11. If the win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs at least one irreversible destination that accumulates progress; otherwise the game becomes pure rearrangement and search churn. A good repair is often to reinterpret an existing receiving pile as a foundation-like sink rather than adding more general-purpose movement.\n12. When a solitaire shows universal search-cap losses with high card and pile usage, the problem is often uncontrolled reversibility rather than lack of moves. Converting one destination type into a one-way, structured sink that matches the win condition usually improves coherence more than adding extra mobility.\n13. If a win condition requires the tableau to empty, the game needs an irreversible sink; otherwise even rich move rules only create churn and search explosion. A good first repair is to convert an already-meaningful pattern in the tableau into a removable/completable unit, rather than broadly loosening movement.\n14. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the game needs an irreversible sink that ordinary exposed cards can actually reach; otherwise rearrangement-only play is provably unwinnable. When repairing a dead design, prefer one core progression route plus one pacing constraint over a broad rewrite.\n15. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the rules need an irreversible outlet that can absorb substantial completed work; otherwise the game becomes pure rearrangement with no path to resolution. When a helper pile is generating lots of branching but no durable progress, repurpose it into a constrained completion zone rather than a general holding area.\n16. If a solitaire already has a natural progress sink, make the win condition measure completion through that sink rather than demanding every auxiliary pile empty by rearrangement alone. Also, when simple bots get trapped by early irreversible promotions, a narrowly targeted undo route can improve practical solvability without flattening the game\u2019s structure.\n17. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires tableau piles to empty, at least one destination type must function as an irreversible progress sink rather than as another rearrangement area. Converting a high-branching helper pile into a foundation-like accumulator often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding extra mobility elsewhere.\n18. When a solitaire repeatedly has 0-move starts, add one broadly useful mobility primitive that is available from the initial deal\u2014such as an empty tableau/cell or a simpler opening build rule\u2014before tuning difficulty. A small structural escape hatch often teaches more about the design than adding many special-case moves.\n19. If a tableau-emptying win condition has no irreversible destination, the game is usually unwinnable no matter how much mobility you add; first create a genuine sink, then tune rearrangement rules around it.\n\nWhen fixing a non-compiling layout, use the arithmetic pass to also check role coverage: every populated pile type should either help reorganize play or accumulate progress toward the win.\n20. If the objective is to empty active piles, the design needs an irreversible sink that those piles can actually feed. A small rule that converts matching pairs into completed cards is often a better first repair than adding more rearrangement moves.\n21. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the rules must provide an irreversible destination that can legally absorb the whole deck. Rearrangement and temporary holding alone create dead games no matter how much local mobility they allow.\n22. When a destination pile is one-way (cards can move into it but not back out), its count and starting state must be compatible with both the deck size and the win condition; otherwise the game can be mathematically unwinnable even if individual moves look reasonable.\n\nA good first repair for a dead solitaire is to align the objective with the actual irreversible progress route already present, rather than adding extra mechanics before the core card flow works.\n23. If a solitaire\u2019s only moves are reversible transfers within the tableau, search-heavy losses usually indicate missing progression rather than mere difficulty. Adding even a simple irreversible sink aligned with a clear tableau build rule can convert aimless branching into meaningful decisions without a full redesign.\n24. If a solitaire asks the player to empty active piles, it needs an irreversible sink that the win condition actually recognizes. When a \u201cfoundation-like\u201d pile exists but victory ignores it, the game often devolves into endless rearrangement rather than resolvable progress.\n25. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible sink that cards can actually reach; otherwise the game is only rearrangement and will often be provably unwinnable. When a pile type is fed by the stock, give it an active role in progress rather than leaving it as a dead receiver.\n26. If the objective requires tableau piles to empty, the game needs an irreversible destination that cards can move into; rearrangement alone only creates churn and search explosion. When a design is failing at the structure level, replacing broad low-value movement with one clear build rule plus a true sink is often more effective than adding more freedom.\n27. When a solitaire shows many 0-use losses, fix the move graph before tuning difficulty: add one broadly available tableau move and one clear sink that cards can actually reach. Also, if the win condition requires emptying piles, make sure some destination type removes cards from circulation in a one-way, progress-measuring manner.\n28. If a win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs both broad circulation out of those piles and an irreversible sink elsewhere; rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying objective. When a game is universally unwinnable with tiny explored state spaces, a small repair that establishes one clear tableau rule plus one clear completion rule is usually more informative than adding many special-case moves.\n29. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition asks play-area piles to become empty, the rules need a reachable sink that can permanently absorb cards; otherwise the game only shuffles cards around without a path to completion.\n\nWhen fixing an invalid objective reference, prefer correcting it in a way that also clarifies each pile type\u2019s role in the overall card flow, rather than making the smallest syntactic patch alone.\n30. When a deck uses only one color, any alternate-color tableau rule silently kills mobility; suit-match, rank-only, or empty-cell style rules are safer starting points. Also, if two move rules target the same pile pair, merging them into one clearer progression rule often both fixes compilation and exposes the game's true structure for playtesting.\n31. If the objective is to empty the tableau, the rules need an irreversible destination that removes cards from circulation; rearrangement alone only creates churn. When search caps are high but wins are zero, adding a simple, conventional sink often reveals whether the rest of the move structure is fundamentally sound.\n32. When a solitaire fails to compile, prefer the smallest structural fix that preserves the intended move graph; getting back to a runnable version is more valuable than speculative balance changes. Broad source sets can often replace duplicated move rules cleanly, reducing both errors and rules noise.\n33. If the objective requires piles to empty, the rules need an irreversible destination that cards can accumulate into. Rearrangement-only systems may generate legal moves and search depth, but they cannot produce actual progress toward an emptying win condition.\n34. When a solitaire has a real sink (like suit-built foundations) but still times out on every run, the problem is often excess reversible tableau freedom rather than insufficient mobility. Tightening the dominant rearrangement rule to better align with the sink can reduce search churn and make progress-oriented decisions matter without requiring a full redesign.\n35. Before tuning difficulty or move rules, make the arithmetic and card flow valid end-to-end. A solitaire that doesn't account for every card can't reveal whether its mechanics are good, because the design never reaches the stage where play can be meaningfully evaluated.\n36. When a move rule depends on a property the deck cannot realize\u2014like alternating colors in an all-black deck\u2014the game can collapse to 0-move starts across most shuffles. Before tuning difficulty, make sure the deck composition, build rules, and win condition form a compatible core loop with at least one genuine sink for progress.\n37. If the win condition requires the tableau to empty, the game needs an irreversible sink; otherwise play degenerates into reversible shuffling with no path to completion. When search-heavy losses dominate, removing a broad backflow route can improve solvability more than adding extra movement freedom, because it turns progress piles into real progress instead of temporary storage.\n38. When a solitaire has no irreversible sink, an \u201cempty the tableau\u201d win condition is often structurally impossible no matter how many rearrangement moves exist. First ensure there is a single, valid progression route into a true destination pile, then make the win condition measure completion of that route rather than depletion of temporary holding areas.\n39. When a solitaire only allows rearrangement among active piles, losses with medium-to-high search counts often indicate \"progressless mobility\" rather than fair difficulty. Adding a clear irreversible sink that matches the win condition is usually a better repair than adding still more movement.\n40. When a game is universally unwinnable with near-zero usage, fix the move graph before adjusting difficulty: the first priority is to ensure every occupied pile can legally feed a true progress sink.\nA tableau rule should express a broadly available relation players can actually satisfy from random starts; exotic predicates that rarely match are effectively no rule at all.\n41. When a game's main progress route is a single permissive destination pile, repeated identical win lengths often mean the play is too linear even if win rate is high. Adding one broadly useful tableau-transfer move can create real decisions and rescue awkward deals without needing a full rules rewrite.\n42. If the objective is to empty tableau-style piles, the rules need a true sink that permanently accumulates progress; otherwise the game produces reversible rearrangement and search explosions instead of solvable structure. When a pile type currently named in the win condition is only receiving cards, it often works better to redefine it as the foundation-like destination and shift the win to clearing the active play areas.\n43. When a solitaire loses only by search-cap across all runs, the problem is often not lack of legal moves but lack of irreversible progress. Adding a clear completion sink and aligning the win condition to that sink usually improves solvability more than adding extra mobility.\n44. If a solitaire\u2019s objective is to empty piles, it needs an irreversible sink; pure rearrangement almost always creates churn instead of progress. A good repair is often to specialize one existing pile type into foundations rather than adding more movement freedom everywhere.\n45. If a win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible destination that actually removes cards from circulation. Broad rearrangement and easy holding areas without a true sink usually produce search-cap losses from aimless churn rather than meaningful difficulty.\n46. If the objective is to empty several piles, the design needs either a genuine removal mechanism or a destination that can absorb essentially the whole deck; a tiny one-way sink creates provable unwinnability no matter how interesting the matching rule is.\n\nWhen a game has only a single \u201ccapture\u201d move, add one broad reorganization move among the active piles before tuning difficulty. Restoring circulation usually reveals whether the core pairing/removal idea is actually playable.\n47. If the win condition requires piles to empty, the design must include an irreversible sink or removal action; rearrangement alone can never convert mobility into progress. When repairing a dead solitaire, first create a coherent flow from source to tableau to sink before tuning difficulty.\n48. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible destination such as foundations or a removal rule; rearrangement alone cannot create progress toward \u201cempty everything.\u201d\n\nWhen repairing an uncompiled design, fix both the arithmetic error and any deeper structural impossibility exposed by the same pass, so the first playable version can produce meaningful evaluation data.\n49. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires tableau piles to empty, it needs an irreversible progress sink; rearrangement-only systems usually create large search spaces without any path to victory.\nTightening the highest-frequency move rule can improve solvability when a bot is drowning in reversible options, especially once a real outlet for progress exists.\n50. When a win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the design needs an actual sink that permanently removes cards from circulation; rearrangement alone only creates churn, not progress.\n\nA good first repair for a dead solitaire is to add one clear progression route that matches familiar card structure (for example, suit-built foundations), before tuning difficulty or adding more freedoms.\n51. If a solitaire\u2019s main special move sends cards to an ordinary pile, that pile is not a real sink unless the win condition explicitly values filling it or otherwise resolving it. When emptiness-based wins are impossible through mere rearrangement, reframe the objective around the actual accumulation pile before adding more mobility.\n52. If the objective requires piles to shrink or clear, the rules need an irreversible sink; rearrangement alone only creates churn and search explosion. When repairing a dead solitaire, adding one clear progress destination is often more effective than adding more tableau mobility.\n53. If a win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible sink; pure rearrangement rarely produces solvable deals no matter how interesting the movement rule is.\nA minimal repair is often to add one completion destination that uses the game\u2019s existing matching logic, rather than broadening tableau freedom and inflating the search space.\n54. If the objective is to empty the tableau, the rules must provide an irreversible sink; rearrangement alone cannot create a win. When a design is universally unwinnable, the first repair should be a minimal structural fix that connects ordinary play to a clear completion route.\n55. Before tuning difficulty, verify that the initial layout accounts for every card exactly once. A solitaire with a one-card arithmetic error can\u2019t reveal anything about its rules, so the most useful early revision is often the smallest compile-fix that preserves the existing design for real playtesting.\n56. When a game is losing on every shuffle with near-zero usage and exhausted searches, the next revision should restore a connected card-flow graph before tuning difficulty: every active pile type needs at least one outbound move, and the win condition needs a reachable sink that irreversibly advances progress.\n57. When a solitaire has a valid completion sink but the bot only hits search limits, look for high-volume reversible moves before adding more freedom. Restricting empty-space moves to a specific rank is a compact way to reduce churn while keeping empty piles tactically valuable.\n58. When a solitaire\u2019s only productive outlet is solid but tableau reorganization happens one card at a time, adding a stack-move that preserves the tableau\u2019s existing build rule can improve solvability and decision quality at once: it reduces reversible churn while making buried-card access more realistic. Favor stack mobility that reinforces the main structure over adding new wildcard spaces or loosening the victory sink.\n59. When a solitaire rules engine forbids duplicate source/destination move definitions, merge the alternatives into one condition tree before changing gameplay. Structural compile fixes should preserve the intended move graph as closely as possible so later playtest data reflects design quality rather than accidental rule drift.\n60. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires piles to empty, rearrangement alone is not enough; the design needs an irreversible sink that cards can flow into under clear progression rules. When search-cap losses come from broad reversible moves, replacing exotic permissions with one coherent tableau rule plus one strict foundation rule often improves both solvability and decision quality.\n61. When a solitaire has many legal rearrangements but no wins, the next fix is often not \u201cmore mobility\u201d but an earlier irreversible outlet. A small progress sink that peels off naturally exposed cards can reduce search-space churn and help both humans and bots convert local organization into durable advancement.\n62. When the engine treats the stock/draw as a special object rather than a normal pile type, avoid referencing it in win conditions or pile-quantifier checks that only accept declared pile types. More broadly, make victory track where cards must end up, not whether a source pile happened to empty.\n63. If a win condition requires multiple pile types to empty, every one of those pile types needs a path into an irreversible sink. Rearrangement-only rules can create activity, but without a completion outlet they make the puzzle provably unwinnable rather than merely difficult.\n64. When a game\u2019s only progress sink is suit-built foundations, the number of foundation piles must be compatible with the deck composition and starting rule. Too few foundations can create hard structural deadlocks that no amount of tableau maneuvering can fix, especially when only one rank can open a pile.\n65. If a win condition requires piles to empty, the game needs an irreversible outlet; a holding pile by itself only creates churn. When a rules engine rejects overlapping move definitions, it's often best to unify them into one broader rule and use structure elsewhere to express progression.\n66. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible destination that removes cards from circulation; otherwise the game often becomes pure rearrangement with a large, fruitless search space.\nTightening tableau movement while adding a clear progression sink usually improves solvability more than adding still more permissive internal moves.\n67. When a win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs at least one irreversible destination that converts organization into progress; otherwise the game often degenerates into endless reversible rearrangement. Reducing high-volume back-and-forth moves can improve solvability more than adding extra freedom, because it reshapes the search space around meaningful progress.\n68. When a solitaire already has lots of legal motion but no wins, the problem is often not lack of branching but lack of assembly power. Loosening tableau-building rules while keeping the final scoring/completion sink strict can preserve structure and decisions while making actual progress reachable.\n69. When most losses are 0\u20131 move and fully exhausted, add a broadly useful mobility tool before tuning difficulty. Small helper piles that both accept and release cards can revive the opening without bypassing the real objective, as long as the win condition requires them to be cleared again.\n70. If the win condition requires piles to empty, at least one pile type must function as an irreversible sink or completion area; pure rearrangement cannot finish the game.\nWhen many deals start with zero legal moves, first broaden one common primitive move that matches the initial layout, rather than adding several special-case escape hatches.\n71. If a solitaire\u2019s objective is to empty its main play piles, the design needs an irreversible sink that cards can flow into; otherwise even rich tableau movement only creates churn. When search-cap losses happen with high pile usage, adding a single coherent completion route is often more informative than loosening tableau rules further.\n72. If losses consistently hit a search cap with moderate pile usage, the problem is often reversible churn rather than raw difficulty. Adding or clarifying a one-way progress sink usually helps more than adding extra mobility, especially when the win condition requires piles to empty.\n73. When a solitaire has many losses at the search cap rather than quick dead-ends, the problem is often excessive reversible stock/tableau churn rather than pure difficulty. A small increase in tableau capacity\u2014paid for by a small reduction in stock\u2014can improve early organization and make the state space more purposeful without changing the core rules.\n74. If a pile can receive cards but not release them, and the win condition does not explicitly require that pile to be resolved, it usually becomes either a dead sink or a loophole. Either make such a pile a true irreversible completion zone that the win counts, or keep it connected to the rest of the move graph so cards placed there still participate in play.\n75. If a win condition requires piles to empty, the game needs an irreversible sink; otherwise even generous movement rules create churn rather than progress. Adding a dedicated completion destination usually improves solvability more than further loosening tableau movement, because it turns rearrangement into durable advancement.\n76. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires the tableau to empty, the design needs an irreversible sink that cards can legally flow into; rearrangement alone cannot produce victory. When repairing a broken prototype, first make every card exist in the setup and give every major pile type a clear role in the win path before tuning difficulty.\n77. If the win condition requires piles to become empty, the game needs an irreversible way for cards to leave circulation. Adding more rearrangement usually only enlarges the search space; a simple \u201ccash-out\u201d destination for completed patterns often fixes unwinnability more effectively than loosening tableau moves.\n78. If cards can only move into a single shared sink, difficulty often comes from lack of circulation rather than meaningful restriction. Adding one simple tableau-to-tableau transfer rule can turn a search-cap stalemate into a playable game far more effectively than loosening the sink itself.\n79. If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible destination that accumulates progress; otherwise the game becomes pure rearrangement and the search space fills with loops instead of solutions.\n\nWhen a solitaire is failing at the search cap rather than exhausting quickly, look for high-volume reversible moves first. Converting one pile type from a general holding area into a structured foundation often improves both solvability and decision quality.\n80. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires piles to empty, at least one pile type must act as an irreversible sink; otherwise the design can generate lots of legal motion without any path to completion. Adding progress destinations usually helps more than adding yet more tableau mobility, especially when search losses show high branching but no wins.\n81. If a pile type only receives cards and never releases them, it should usually be treated as a sink and the win condition should reward filling it or emptying the active tableau instead of asking that sink itself to become empty. When a solitaire only allows rearrangement plus dead-end deposits, adding a true irreversible destination often fixes both unwinnability and aimless search more effectively than loosening tableau moves.\n82. When a solitaire loses in 0\u20133 moves with fully exhausted searches, the next step should be to restore a connected move graph, not to tweak difficulty. In empty-the-tableau games, add both a reorganization route among active piles and a one-way progress sink; either one alone is usually insufficient.\n83. When a solitaire uses a one-suit deck, suit-based stack-validity checks can become redundant friction rather than meaningful structure. Removing redundant constraints is often a better first difficulty adjustment than adding new mechanics, because it improves mobility without changing the game\u2019s core objective or card flow.\n84. If the win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs an irreversible sink; otherwise the game becomes pure rearrangement and search-space churn rather than progress.\nWhen a bot consistently times out instead of exhausting, look for broad reversible moves between non-sink piles and replace them with a tighter progression route.\n85. When a game is unwinnable because the only \u201cprogress\u201d destination is too restrictive, first make that destination a genuine sink with a simple, repeatable acceptance rule. At the same time, reduce high-branching rearrangement moves so tableau motion serves exposure and consolidation rather than reversible churn.\n86. If the objective is to empty tableau piles, the game needs a real one-way sink that cards can enter under a completion rule and not need to leave again. Helper piles that only accumulate random cards without matching the win condition usually make a game provably unwinnable rather than merely difficult.\n87. When a solitaire is mostly winnable but has a small number of fully proven dead deals, a good repair is often to give an existing destination pile a second, controlled role rather than adding a whole new subsystem. Reusing foundations or similar piles as limited emergency workspace can eliminate hard locks while preserving the game\u2019s main structure.\n88. When a solitaire has decent apparent win rate but extremely long solutions and many search-cap losses, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than insufficient freedom. Tightening the loosest high-traffic move so destinations enforce meaningful structure can improve decision quality and reduce search churn without a full redesign.\n89. When losses consistently hit search limits with high card usage, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of mobility. A strong repair is to introduce a true one-way sink that matches the win condition, and to tighten the busiest move route so tableau moves organize cards toward that sink instead of merely expanding the state space.\n90. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the game needs an irreversible sink that actually removes cards from circulation; rearrangement and one-way holding piles are not enough. When a helper pile can receive cards but not release them or score completed structures, it usually acts as dead weight unless the objective explicitly gives it a productive role.\n91. When a solitaire fails basic deck accounting, fix that first with the smallest addition that also preserves card flow. If you add a stock/draw to absorb missing cards, make sure its exposed cards can enter the main play loop immediately; otherwise you only replace a compile error with a dead source pile.\n92. When a solitaire\u2019s only irreversible progress requires completing a long perfect stack in one move, search often collapses into reversible churn. Adding a simpler incremental sink\u2014such as standard one-card foundation building\u2014can dramatically improve solvability while preserving the tableau\u2019s decision structure.\n93. When losses mostly hit a search cap rather than exhausting quickly, the problem is often excessive reversible motion rather than pure difficulty. Adding a clear irreversible sink that matches the win condition usually improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding more mobility.\n94. If the win condition is \u201cempty these source piles,\u201d the design needs an irreversible destination that cards can move into under a clear progression rule. Rearrangement-only systems often look active in testing but collapse into search-space churn because nothing converts mobility into lasting progress.\n95. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the rules must include an irreversible outlet for cards; pure rearrangement cannot produce victory no matter how much tactical freedom the tableau has.\n\nWhen repairing an unwinnable solitaire, prefer adding one clear completion mechanism that matches the existing build rule over broadening movement everywhere; it reveals the game\u2019s real difficulty without exploding the search space.\n96. If a solitaire\u2019s goal is to empty working piles, the rules must include an irreversible sink that cards can move into; otherwise the state space is just rearrangement with no path to resolution. When a file fails before simulation, fix the structural compile issue and the missing progression mechanic together so the next test measures gameplay rather than parser fragility.\n97. When a game wins at move 0, fix the objective before tuning mechanics: otherwise playtest data tells you nothing about the actual move system. A good first repair is to make victory depend on clearing or resolving the piles that already contain the cards in play.\n98. Dead-end helper piles are especially harmful when the win condition requires the source area to empty: any card moved into a non-progressing sink becomes permanent anti-progress. When a game already has a meaningful completion destination, prefer strengthening access to that destination over adding extra storage.\n99. When a solitaire repeatedly has 0-move or near-0-move starts, fix the existence of a usable progress sink before tuning difficulty. A foundation or removal route that is too restrictive for the layout can make an otherwise reasonable tableau rule set completely unwinnable.\n100. If the win condition requires play-area piles to empty, the rules need at least one irreversible destination that can accumulate cards permanently. Rearrangement-only systems are often provably unwinnable no matter how much mobility they allow.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, and impossible predicates as first-order design failures. Fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable, faithful version before tuning difficulty.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build relations must be realizable by the actual deck and layout; exotic or mismatched predicates are effectively no rule at all.\n- Repair the move graph before balancing challenge. Universal 0-move starts, near-zero usage, or tiny exhausted searches usually mean active piles lack a common opening transfer or cannot reach any progress route.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: restore one coherent source \u2192 tableau \u2192 sink loop first, so playtests measure gameplay structure instead of parser errors or disconnected rules.\n\n## Align card flow with victory\n- If the win condition requires tableau or other active piles to empty, the game needs a reachable irreversible sink: foundations, removals, completed-set cash-outs, or another one-way destination. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying objective.\n- The win condition should measure real resolution of the play area, not depletion of bookkeeping sources like stock/draw or temporary holding zones.\n- If a pile can receive cards but not release them, it must either count as completed work or be kept out of the critical flow. One-way receiving piles that the objective does not reward usually become dead sinks, blockers, or loopholes.\n- Sink capacity and structure must match the deck and objective. Too few destinations, over-restrictive opening rules, or sinks that cannot absorb the whole required workload can make a game mathematically unwinnable.\n- Prefer incremental, steady progress sinks over \u201call-at-once\u201d completion gates. Designs are more solvable when ordinary exposed cards can convert organization into durable advancement.\n- Every populated pile type should have a clear role in the win path: expose cards, reorganize them, or accumulate irreversible progress.\n\n## Shape mobility and search space\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use points to missing mobility or disconnected flow; search-cap losses with moderate-to-high use usually point to excessive reversible freedom or missing irreversible progress.\n- When branching is high but wins are absent, tighten the busiest reversible move routes before adding more freedom. Restricting empty-space use, removing backflow from progress areas, or making destinations encode stronger structure often improves solvability.\n- Add mobility in broad, structural ways, not with many exceptions. One useful tableau transfer, stack move, or limited helper space is usually better than several special-case escape hatches.\n- Favor movement rules that reinforce the game\u2019s main structure. Stack moves should preserve the tableau\u2019s build logic, and tableau rules should organize cards toward the sink rather than create aimless circulation.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should either support reorganization temporarily and be clearable again, or be repurposed into constrained completion zones.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small, compatible changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in otherwise workable games, improve opening mobility with the narrowest broadly available rule that fits the design, such as a simple tableau relation or rank-limited move to empties.\n- If the game already has a real sink but play feels too linear or awkward, add one general reorganization route among active piles before loosening the sink itself.\n- Small layout shifts can matter when rules are sound: increasing early tableau capacity or reducing stock gating can improve organization and reveal the real decision space, but only if the stock actually feeds play.\n- When a game already has a natural accumulation area, prefer aligning the win condition to that progress route over adding new subsystems. Reuse existing meaningful pile roles before inventing more mechanics.\n\n## Express rules cleanly\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine dislikes duplicate source/destination rules, merge related permissions into one clear condition tree or broader source set instead of scattering near-duplicate rules.\n- Use syntax fixes to clarify structure, not just to silence errors: after every compile repair, re-check that each rule still contributes to a coherent progression system.\n```"}]